WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.910
Here's one short program, with
two mistakes hiding in it.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.890
Hand it to one tool, and
before anything runs,

3
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.090
you get a list of both mistakes.

4
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.150
Hand it to another, and
the first few lines run.

5
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.530
Then it stops at the first mistake.

6
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.930
Same code, same mistakes.

7
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.230
So why does one tool show you everything,

8
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.030
and the other just one?

9
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.650
In this mission, you'll see
why code must be translated:

10
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.050
the CPU can only execute machine code.

11
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.290
You'll meet the two kinds of
translator for high-level code:

12
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.650
a compiler and an interpreter.

13
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.290
Then you'll work out which tool was which.

14
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.250
Your mission: explain why some
languages list every mistake,

15
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.760
and others stop at one.
